      en: "The Ingoré Old School was built in 1936 during Portuguese colonial rule, one of the first official schools in the interior. The mud-brick building with wooden windows has two classrooms and a teacher's house. Up to 1974, about 1,000 students were taught there. After independence, the school fell into disrepair due to lack of government funds. In 2005, it was temporarily repaired by a local NGO. Today it is occasionally used for literacy classes. The school symbolizes the educational heritage of the region.",
